[
    {
        "content": "<p>A friend shared a Haskell idiom:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>for :: Functor f =&gt; f a -&gt; ( a -&gt; b ) -&gt; f b\nfor fa g = fmap g fa\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>For example, the expression <code>for [1, 2, 3] square</code> has the value <code>[1, 4, 9]</code>. This is a \"for each expression\", and it's relatively common in software.</p>\n<p>It occured to me the yoneda lemma amounts to saying the first arrow in the above definition is an isomorphism</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F a \\cong \\mathrm{hom}( y(a), F )</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">hom</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>Has anyone noticed this before? I'm surprised I haven't seen this connection pointed out to me yet.</p>\n<p>It seems \"readily intuitionable\", like the expression</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{for} \\begin{bmatrix}\n    0 &amp; 1 \\\\\n    1 &amp; 0\n  \\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.4em;vertical-align:-0.95em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">for</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\"><span class=\"mopen delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mtable\"><span class=\"col-align-c\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.45em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.61em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.41em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.95em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"col-align-c\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.45em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.61em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.41em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.95em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose delimcenter\" style=\"top:0em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size3\">]</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>Could stand for the natural transformation that flips matricies with two rows.</p>\n<p>Anybody seen this explored before?</p>",
        "id": 643396554,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Kreitzberg",
        "timestamp": 1795556197
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>the \"do notation\" / \"for notation\" / \"comprehension notation\" that is common in Haskell requires a strong monad, with a plus and zero additionally interpreting unions and empty structures.  It may work with matrices.  The interplay between the various collections monads among themselves, and what happens when you unpack the collection monads into things like folds, is explored in functional query processing.  <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1008705026446\">https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1008705026446</a></p>",
        "id": 643398064,
        "sender_full_name": "Ryan Wisnesky",
        "timestamp": 1795556646
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I did see <code>forM_ :: (Foldable t, Monad m) =&gt; t a -&gt; (a -&gt; m b) -&gt; m ()</code>, and <code>forM :: (Traversable t, Monad m) =&gt; t a -&gt; (a -&gt; m b) -&gt; m (t b)</code>. I caught the later assigned a value to \"the entire loop\" whereas the former relied on side effects. I wasn't sure if boiling out all that data \"lost the essence\" of \"for\".</p>\n<p>Maybe a question I could've asked myself is whether that first arrow of <code>forM</code> is still an isomorphism. It's almost implemented the same way.</p>",
        "id": 643403248,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Kreitzberg",
        "timestamp": 1795558118
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess you start with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall a . \\forall b . fa \\to (a \\to b) \\to fb</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\">.∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>, and then you argue that this can be regarded as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall a . fa \\to [\\forall b . (a \\to b) \\to fb]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> which is where the iso comes into play? After fixing a type <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>This also gives you co-yoneda if you can recognise that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall a . \\forall b . fa \\to (a \\to b) \\to fb</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\">.∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span> is the same as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall b . \\forall a . fa \\to (a \\to b) \\to fb</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">.∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>, is the same as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall b . (\\exists a . fa \\times (a \\to b)) \\to fb</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">))</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>.</p>",
        "id": 643421298,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruby Khondaker (she/her)",
        "timestamp": 1795563995
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes that's right, that's my understanding of it.</p>",
        "id": 643428492,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Kreitzberg",
        "timestamp": 1795567010
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>yes that idiom is functoriality of functors, which is what Yoneda is about</p>",
        "id": 643486527,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Capucci (he/him)",
        "timestamp": 1795590332
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>indeed <code>for</code> is defined in terms of <code>fmap</code>, which is literally functoriality</p>",
        "id": 643486693,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Capucci (he/him)",
        "timestamp": 1795590392
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Should I think of the usual yoneda as just interchanging the arguments of the functor's arrow mapping structure?</p>\n<p>I guess can you expand on what you mean by \"yoneda is about functoriality of functors\"? I usually think of yoneda as being about representable functors.</p>",
        "id": 643488009,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Kreitzberg",
        "timestamp": 1795590910
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"277352\">Alex Kreitzberg</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/229199-learning.3A-questions/topic/For.20loops.20and.20the.20yoneda.20lemma/near/643488009\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I guess can you expand on what you mean by \"yoneda is about functoriality of functors\"? I usually think of yoneda as being about representable functors.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>a presheaf on C is a left C-module, functoriality is the 'C-action'. Yoneda is the observation that a representable is a free left C-module on a generator of the form <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a:1 \\to C_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, thus a map of modules <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ya \\to F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> is the same thing as a map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\to |F|</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∣</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord\">∣</span></span></span></span> , where <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> is just the assignment on objects of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span>, and such a map is trivial on all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b \\in C_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0715em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> except on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span>, where it picks out the elements of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fa</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span>.<br>\nthe free-forgetful adjunction which underlies this principle works because <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> is functorial &amp; the maps we consider between functors are natural.</p>",
        "id": 643577789,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Capucci (he/him)",
        "timestamp": 1795613090
    }
]